  [case id=sched-037-recur-skip-large-advance-fires-once level=core
   [tags sched recur on-missed skip large-advance perf hostile]
  [; #853 (§6.4.1 — construction is operand-consuming) — outcomes are collected in
     a paren SEQUENCE, not a labelling element. A computed [err] in element CHILD
     position propagates, so `[r1 $e]` would short-circuit the construction and
     destroy the evidence this case exists to show. Position carries what the
     labels carried; the full err VALUE stays asserted, so nothing is weakened. ]
   [doc [#
HOSTILE (blocker): an unbounded :skip recur advanced across ~50000 occurrences
must fire EXACTLY ONCE and return immediately. The prior sch_fire_recur walked
time-next-occurrence once PER crossed occurrence even for :skip/:coalesce
(which fire once), so this advance took minutes (O(N) in the missed count). It
now jumps straight to the next occurrence after the target in a single
fast-forwarding call; if the O(N) walk is reintroduced this case hangs the gate.
#]]
   [in-cx [# [empty] #]]
   [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/sched']
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/time']
[?let [= $ch [?channel name="t" buffer=16]]
[= $r [$time:recurrence :daily '1970-01-01T00:00:00Z' 'UTC' {}]]
[= $t [$sched:recur $r $ch {on-missed: :skip}]]
[= $_ [$sched:test-clock-advance [$time:duration-d 50000]]]
[= $a [?receive from=$ch]]
[= $b [?receive from=$ch]]
($a, $b)]
#]]
   [out-text [#
([tick timer=timer-1], [err code=cx-err:CXER0202 message='receive timed out'])
#]]
  ]

  [case id=sched-038-restore-no-duplicate-timer level=core
   [tags sched restore durable dedup worker-recycle hostile]
  [; #853 (§6.4.1 — construction is operand-consuming) — outcomes are collected in
     a paren SEQUENCE, not a labelling element. A computed [err] in element CHILD
     position propagates, so `[r1 $e]` would short-circuit the construction and
     destroy the evidence this case exists to show. Position carries what the
     labels carried; the full err VALUE stays asserted, so nothing is weakened. ]
   [doc [#
HOSTILE (major): restore re-arms a durable intent by name, but the registry was
keyed by a fresh monotonic id, so re-arming a name already live left a
DUPLICATE timer (both fired, doubling delivery, §2.4 worker-recycle wrong).
sched-024 only receives once so it never saw the duplicate; here we arm 'win',
restore it, advance, and receive TWICE: exactly ONE tick must arrive (the
second receive times out). Name is now the registry identity.
#]]
   [in-cx [# [empty] #]]
   [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/sched']
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/journal']
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/time']
[?let [= $ch [?channel name="t" buffer=8]]
[= $jr [$journal:open "mem://sched" "tenant"]]
[= $t [$sched:after [$time:duration-m 10] $ch {name: 'win' durable: $jr}]]
[= $rep [$sched:restore $jr {'win': $ch}]]
[= $_ [$sched:test-clock-advance [$time:duration-m 10]]]
[= $a [?receive from=$ch]]
[= $b [?receive from=$ch]]
($a, $b)]
#]]
   [out-text [#
([tick timer=win], [err code=cx-err:CXER0202 message='receive timed out'])
#]]
  ]

  [case id=sched-039-tick-reaches-a-fan-out-subscriber level=core
   [tags sched timer channel fan-out regression sup-1e]
   [doc [#
REGRESSION (SUP-1e class): a timer armed on a FAN-OUT channel
(sharing=independent) delivered its tick through the GROUP queue, where no
subscriber ever looks — a dead wire, invisible because nothing had ever armed
a timer at a fan-out point until the supervisor module did. The tick must
reach a subscriber's window like any other fan-out send. Pinned directly at
the sched layer so the guarantee does not rest on supervise's fixtures alone.
#]]
   [in-cx [# [empty] #]]
   [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/sched']
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/time']
[?let [= $ch [?channel name="fanout-tick" sharing=independent retention=window keep=4]]
[= $sub [?subscribe from=$ch]]
[= $t [$sched:after [$time:duration-m 5] $ch {name: 'fan'}]]
[= $_ [$sched:test-clock-advance [$time:duration-m 5]]]
[= $r [?receive from=$sub]]
$r]
#]]
   [out-text [#
[tick timer=fan]
#]]
  ]
]
